Under the documented rule, `recover_json_object` scores "the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one", and "a candidate that parses as strict JSON, or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable". For the input `fence[ A ]`, the fence holds A, so the rule's recovered object is A (last-wins `{"a":2}`). For the rewritten `fence[ A ] B`, the fence still holds A, so the rule again scores A, not the later B. Therefore the rule says the recovery kernel is unchanged. The code instead returns B for the rewritten text because its fenced pool skips duplicate-key candidates and then takes the last top-level object.